{"labs": [{"stimuli": [{"id": 0, "template_override": 1, "type": "welcome", "contents": [{}], "_data": []}, {"id": 1, "type": "audio", "title": "", "contents": [{"type": "slideshow", "value": {"audiosrc": "enpe7n1le01ob04re2aud01", "coverimg": "enpe7n1le01ob04re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"id": 0, "image": "enpe7n1le01ob04re2img01.jpg", "text": "<div align=\"justify\">I always get questions about programming algorithms, so let's start this session by giving a fast but precise explanation on this topic. I do this because most of the people come to my class thinking this is a complicated topic, but believe me when I say programming algorithms is not difficult.</br>\nAn algorithm is like a numbered list of things you need to do to get a result. A programming algorithm is the same, but the procedure is described by using a programming language, which is the one the computer can understand. The computer receives the input that is the information needed (the steps) to develop the procedure. Then, when the computer does the procedure, an outcome is expected; the outcome is the final result of the procedure. Let's imagine you are cooking using the help of a recipe. After following each step, the outcome is the finished dish.</div>", "time": "100"}, {"id": 1, "image": "enpe7n1le01ob04re2img02.jpg", "text": "<div align=\"justify\">When doing this procedure all the steps have to be very clear; there is no room for interpretation when writing algorithms. You can't make a formula to bake an apple pie and as a result, have a chocolate cake. When something like this happens, it is because the instructions are not precise, they are ambiguous, and that can't happen on programming algorithms.</br>\nAs I said before, an algorithm is a list, but you can also do it as a flowchart. Just in case, let's have a little review on what that is. In this case,when writing the, algorithm you need the same steps just that you use shapes and arrows, creating a map route to the desired result. In other words, with a flowchart, you can visualize the route you are taking to solve a problem on your computer.</div>", "time": "61000"}, {"id": 2, "image": "enpe7n1le01ob04re2img03.jpg", "text": "<div align=\"justify\">Let's see an example of a flowchart for an email password. This kind of flowchart is made of several symbols that have specific purposes. The Terminal or Terminator, which indicates where the process starts or finishes. The rectangle, which represents the Process Object and usually contains a verb that specifies the action to be taken; in the case of the email password, it usually contains the verb <i>change</i> or even the words <i>error message</i>, in case the user makes a mistake when entering the information. We also have the Data Object  or I/O shape, a parallelogram which shows the inputs and outputs from a process; for the email password it contains the words <i>Old password</i>, <i>New password</i>, and <i>Verify password</i>. Finally, there is the Decision/Conditional Object, which is represented as a diamond with a question or statement in it and two arrows coming out of it representing its two possible answers, yes or no; for our example, these diamonds contain the sentences <i> \u201cLoad data to form\u201d </i>,<i> \u201cIs validation data?\u201d </i> and <i> \u201cUpdate password to database\u201d </i>.</div>", "time": "117500"}]}}], "template_override": 1, "_data": []}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar", "exercises": [{"id": 0, "type": "WLC", "questions": [], "stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "img_path": "", "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "I can say what a programming algorithm is. ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can discuss a simple example of an algorithm in a numbered list.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can talk about a simple example of an algorithm in a flow chart.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "When you want to reach a goal in your life, do you follow procedures? Why? Why not?", 